          "secNumber": "2.6",
          "heading": "Asymptotic Normality and the Finite Population Central Limit Theorem",
          "content": "While the design unbiasedness of $\\bar{y}$ and $s^2$ holds exactly for any finite sample size $n \\ge 2$, constructing valid inferential confidence intervals and conducting hypothesis tests requires knowledge of the asymptotic sampling distribution of the standardized error:\n$$Z_n = \\frac{\\bar{y} - \\bar{Y}}{\\sqrt{V(\\bar{y})}} = \\frac{\\bar{y} - \\bar{Y}}{\\sqrt{\\frac{1 - f}{n}} S}$$\n\nIn classical mathematical statistics, the Lindeberg–Lévy Central Limit Theorem applies to independent and identically distributed random variables. Under SRSWOR, however, the observations are statistically dependent because sampling is conducted without replacement, and the population itself is finite. Formal asymptotic theory for finite populations requires formulating a sequence of nested finite populations $\\{U_\\nu\\}_{\\nu=1}^\\infty$ with increasing sizes $N_\\nu \\to \\infty$ and corresponding sample sizes $n_\\nu \\to \\infty$.\n\nThe definitive mathematical conditions for asymptotic normality in finite population sampling were established by Jaroslav Hájek in his seminal 1960 paper. Hájek proved that $Z_n \\xrightarrow{d} \\mathcal{N}(0, 1)$ as $n_\\nu \\to \\infty$ and $N_\\nu - n_\\nu \\to \\infty$ if and only if the population sequence satisfies the **Hájek Lindeberg-type Condition**:\n$$\\lim_{\\nu \\to \\infty} \\frac{\\sum_{i \\in U_{\\nu, \\tau}} (y_{\\nu i} - \\bar{Y}_\\nu)^2}{(N_\\nu - 1) S_\\nu^2} = 0 \\quad \\forall \\tau > 0$$\nwhere the summation index set $U_{\\nu, \\tau}$ consists of all population units whose standardized deviations exceed the threshold:\n$$U_{\\nu, \\tau} = \\left\\{ i \\in U_\\nu : \\frac{|y_{\\nu i} - \\bar{Y}_\\nu|}{\\sqrt{1 - f_\\nu} \\sqrt{n_\\nu} S_\\nu} > \\tau \\right\\}$$\n\n### Theoretical Interpretation of the Hájek Condition\nThe Hájek condition establishes that asymptotic normality holds provided no single individual population element or microscopic cluster of extreme outliers dominates the total finite population variance. Specifically:\n1. **Vanishing Extremes**: The maximum individual contribution to the total variance, $\\max_{i \\in U} (y_i - \\bar{Y})^2 / \\sum_{j=1}^N (y_j - \\bar{Y})^2$, must vanish asymptotically as $N \\to \\infty$.\n2. **Behavior of the Sampling Fraction**: The theorem requires that $N_\\nu - n_\\nu \\to \\infty$. If the sample size approaches the population size so rapidly that $N_\\nu - n_\\nu = O(1)$, the remaining unobserved population is finite, and the sampling distribution degenerates into a discrete distribution rather than a continuous Gaussian bell curve.\n3. **Studentization Robustness**: In empirical practice, the true standard error is replaced by the sample-based estimate $\\text{se}(\\bar{y}) = \\sqrt{\\frac{1 - f}{n}} s$. By Slutsky’s theorem, because $s^2 / S^2 \\xrightarrow{p} 1$ under mild fourth-moment regularity conditions ($\\frac{1}{N}\\sum (y_i - \\bar{Y})^4 / S^4 < \\infty$), the Studentized statistic:\n   $$T_n = \\frac{\\bar{y} - \\bar{Y}}{\\sqrt{\\frac{1 - f}{n}} s} \\xrightarrow{d} \\mathcal{N}(0, 1)$$\nThis justifies the construction of standard $100(1 - \\alpha)\\%$ Wald asymptotic confidence intervals:\n$$\\text{CI}_{1-\\alpha}(\\bar{Y}) = \\left[ \\bar{y} - z_{\\alpha/2} \\sqrt{\\frac{1 - f}{n}} s, \\; \\bar{y} + z_{\\alpha/2} \\sqrt{\\frac{1 - f}{n}} s \\right]$$\n\nWhen the underlying population exhibits severe skewness (such as enterprise revenue or personal wealth distributions) and the sample size is moderate, the coverage probability of the Wald interval can fall noticeably below the nominal level $1 - \\alpha$. In such environments, survey statisticians construct log-transformed confidence intervals or deploy finite population empirical likelihood intervals to restore nominal coverage."
